PUMP PRICE BOARD

Pump Price Board — a real-world task using only today's concepts: creating a dictionary, reading a value by key, updating a value, adding a new key, get() for missing keys, comparisons, if / elif / else, variables, input(), float(), and printing with 2-decimal money format. No imports, no tricks.

Solve it individually, then come together as a group to compare approaches. Good luck!

A filling station on East-West Road keeps its pump prices on a small board. Each pump has a letter, and the manager wants a program that answers questions about prices without reading the whole board.

The program starts with three pumps already set: A sells at 1100, C at 1115, D at 1090 per litre. Then it must:

- Ask "Which pump: " and print its price: Pump A sells at N1100.00 per litre. If the pump is not on the board (like B), use get() to print: No pump B on this board - without crashing.
- Ask "Which pump is changing: " and its new price, then update that pump and print the updated line for it.
- A new pump has just been installed: ask its letter and price, add it to the board, and print its line.
- End by printing any three pumps the manager asks for, one line each, using the same sentence as the first lookup (missing pumps still get the No pump message).

Sample run:

Which pump: A
Pump A sells at N1100.00 per litre
Which pump is changing: D
New price for D: 1150
Pump D sells at N1150.00 per litre
New pump letter: B
Price for B: 1105
Pump B sells at N1105.00 per litre
Final check - pump 1: D
Pump D sells at N1150.00 per litre

Edge cases to handle: looking up a pump that was never added (message, no crash), a new price with decimals like 1105.50, adding a pump with the same letter as an existing one (it replaces the old price - that is an update, not a crash), and checking get() is what saves you from the missing-key crash.
